# The package index every install below resolves its names against.
#
# Split out of `repos`, which is two concerns: replacing the installer's
# sources with chosen mirrors, which only a machine someone sits at wants, and
# having a current index, which every machine that installs anything needs.
# Both were gated on `workstation`, so a rented host installed nginx against
# whatever index its provider's image happened to carry — empty on most cloud
# images, months old on the rest.
#
# No capability, no `when`, and one payload per system package manager the
# tree knows: no registry entry would ever want a stale index, and neither
# platform should be covered by accident of what the other's `repos` script
# happens to do. pipx, cargo and flatpak need no equivalent — each resolves
# against its remote as it installs and keeps no local index to go stale.
#
# No `requires: repos`: standing on its own is the entire point of the split.
#
# `unless: false` is this vocabulary's way of saying "never already
# satisfied". There is nothing honest to probe — a current index is what this
# unit *does*, and every cheaper answer is a freshness window nobody chose —
# while an action unit with *no* probe is state-cached after its first
# success, which would refresh the index once in a machine's life. So it runs
# every time. That is redundant only on a run where `repos` ran too; on every
# run after that `repos` is itself `cached`, and this is the only thing left
# keeping the index current.
kind: action
root: yes
unless: false
